QCA Windows CNG plugin
----------------------

qca-wincrypto provides QCA algorithms through Windows Cryptography API:
Next Generation (CNG, bcrypt.dll). It needs no third-party libraries and
is built only on Windows. Windows 10 or later is the supported target;
the plugin uses only CNG functions that exist since Windows 7.

Provider name: qca-wincrypto

Features
--------

  random                          BCryptGenRandom, system-preferred RNG
  md5 sha1 sha256 sha384 sha512   CNG hashes
  sha3_256 sha3_384 sha3_512      only where CNG has SHA-3 (Windows 11
                                  24H2 and later); detected at run time
  hmac(md5) hmac(sha1) hmac(sha256) hmac(sha384) hmac(sha512)
  pbkdf2(sha1) pbkdf2(sha256) pbkdf2(sha512)
                                  BCryptDeriveKeyPBKDF2
  hkdf(sha256)                    RFC 5869 on top of CNG HMAC
  aes128-* aes192-* aes256-*      modes ecb, cbc, cbc-pkcs7, cfb, ofb,
                                  ctr, gcm

The AES block function is CNG's. The modes of operation are implemented in
aesmodes.cpp on top of CNG AES-ECB, because CNG's own chaining modes cannot
serve QCA's cipher API: CNG has no OFB or CTR, its CFB takes only whole
blocks, and its GCM takes only 96-bit nonces and whole blocks between
calls. The modes follow QCA's OpenSSL provider: CFB with 128-bit feedback,
CTR with a 128-bit big-endian counter, GCM with any IV length and a tag of
up to 16 bytes. GCM decryption releases no plaintext before the tag has
been verified at final(). aesmodes.cpp has no Windows dependency, so it
can be checked against other implementations on any platform.

Not provided: public-key algorithms (RSA, DSA, DH, EC, Ed25519), X.509,
CMS and TLS. CNG has no EdDSA at all, so Ed25519 stays with qca-ossl.

Building
--------

Enable the plugin like any other:

  cmake -DBUILD_PLUGINS="ossl;wincrypto" ...

or -DWITH_wincrypto_PLUGIN=yes. With BUILD_PLUGINS=auto or all it is
built on Windows and skipped elsewhere.

The unit tests run every algorithm against every loaded provider, so the
regular ctest run on Windows covers this plugin too.
